# Data Architecture

## Core Principles

The browser must not call FRED directly.

External-provider details must remain behind the server-side data layer.

The client should consume an application-owned response format so that FRED can later be supplemented or replaced by direct BLS, BEA, Census, Treasury, or other agency integrations.

## Canonical Series Model

Create a normalized series model resembling:

```ts
export interface EconomicSeries {
  id: string;
  provider: string;
  providerSeriesId: string;
  title: string;
  shortTitle: string;
  description: string;
  question: string;
  units: string;
  frequency: "daily" | "weekly" | "monthly" | "quarterly" | "annual";
  seasonalAdjustment: string | null;
  transformation: string;
  sourceName: string;
  sourceUrl: string;
  observationStart: string | null;
  observationEnd: string | null;
  lastFetchedAt: string;
  observations: EconomicObservation[];
}

export interface EconomicObservation {
  date: string;
  value: number | null;
  realtimeStart?: string | null;
  realtimeEnd?: string | null;
}
```

This is an example, not a mandatory exact shape. Improve it where appropriate.

The model must clearly distinguish:

- The economic period represented by an observation
- When the data was fetched
- The original data provider
- The original series identifier
- The displayed units
- Any transformation applied
- Seasonal-adjustment status

Do not use a generic data shape such as:

```ts
{ x: string, y: number }
```

as the application’s canonical domain model. That shape may be created only at the chart-adapter boundary.

