# Acceptance Criteria

The epic is complete only when all of the following are true.

## Repository

- A Git repository exists.
- `main` contains a clean, reviewable history.
- No secrets are present in tracked files or Git history.
- `.env.example` documents all required variables.

## Local development

- A new developer can follow the README and start the project.
- The application starts with a documented command.
- The local database can be created through committed migrations.
- Seed data or refresh tooling makes local chart rendering reproducible.

## Data

- The application retrieves a real GDP series from FRED.
- External responses are validated.
- Missing observations are handled safely.
- Series metadata is stored.
- Observations are stored.
- Refresh is idempotent.
- Failed refreshes do not erase valid stored data.

## API

- A client-facing endpoint returns normalized GDP data.
- The endpoint has stable success and error shapes.
- The endpoint exposes no secrets.
- The endpoint serves cached data when the external provider is unavailable.

## User interface

- The production dashboard displays the GDP chart.
- The chart answers a clearly stated economic question.
- The chart displays year-over-year real GDP growth.
- Time-range controls work.
- The latest observation and value are visible.
- Units, frequency, transformation, source, observation date, and update time are visible.
- “What this tells you” and “What this does not tell you” are present.
- Loading, error, empty, and stale states exist.
- The layout works on desktop and mobile widths.
- Keyboard focus is visible.
- The chart has an accessible nonvisual representation or summary.

## Quality

- TypeScript strict mode passes.
- Linting passes.
- Unit tests pass.
- Component tests pass.
- The Playwright smoke test passes.
- Production build succeeds.
- No uncaught console errors occur during the primary flow.

## Deployment

- A production Netlify URL exists.
- The deployed application renders stored GDP data.
- Server-side environment variables are configured.
- The FRED API key is absent from the browser bundle.
- The data-refresh mechanism is deployed or fully documented and production-ready.

